# Line endings are decided here, not by whoever happens to be building.
#
# 0.1.0 shipped with CRLF in every one of its 21 wheel files and 26 sdist
# files, because it was built on a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true. That
# breaks nothing — Python reads either — but it means one commit produces two
# different artifacts depending on the machine, `glitch install` writes CRLF
# files into repositories that may lint for them, and the GLITCH-RECEIPT digest
# changes with the builder rather than with the installation. A package that
# asks people to paste a digest should not have the digest depend on who built
# the wheel.
#
# This normalises the working tree to LF on every platform, so a Windows
# checkout, a Linux checkout and the CI runner all build byte-identical
# distributions. Nothing in the repository changes: the stored content was
# always LF, and only the checkout was being rewritten.
* text=auto eol=lf
